      },
      "block": {
        "type": "explanation",
        "label": "RELATIVE CLAUSES · WHO, WHICH, THAT, WHERE",
        "html": "We use relative clauses to give <b>more information</b> and to make <b>longer sentences</b>.<br><br><span style=\"background-color: #CFE9EA\"><b>who</b></span> → people: <i>The people <b>who</b> live here are friendly.</i><br><span style=\"background-color: #FFE2B8\"><b>which</b></span> → things and animals: <i>It's a city <b>which</b> is on an island.</i><br><span style=\"background-color: #E8E0F5\"><b>that</b></span> → people or things (informal): <i>a neighborhood <b>that</b> is near the lake</i><br><span style=\"background-color: #DFF0D8\"><b>where</b></span> → places: <i>a square <b>where</b> people play music</i><br><br>💡 Two sentences → one: <i>I have a friend. <u>She</u> lives in Rio.</i> → <i>I have a friend <b>who</b> lives in Rio.</i> (don't repeat <i>she</i>!)"
      }
    },
